\begin_body

\begin_layout Title
Pseudocode for Developmental windows and Demography
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Main question with examples
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The main question is, given the predictability and severety of food limitations
 in an organism's environment, over what length of time do individuals invest
 in developing higher reproductive quality at an opportunity cost to investing
 in growth to exploit periods of abundant food?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Developmental windows are periods in development where sensitivity to cues
 is hightened.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Walasek et al.
 (2021), Walasek et al.
 (2022) find that the timing of when sensitive periods emerge depends on
 the reliability of cues increases across ontogeny, and their results suggest
 that both information and phenotypic states are modelled, with incremental
 development in fluctuating environments farouring residual development
 across ontogeny.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Further, Walasek et al (2024) review current theory and empirical literature
 and suggest future models could better integrate with empirical research
 by incorporating phenomena found in empirical systems into our models,
 and design more models that can be tailored to the life histories of specific
 species.
 They encourage for instance that models that incorporate the life history
 trade-offs we see in the development of alternative phenotypes in bulb
 mites (between scramblers and fighters) (Smallegange, 2023; Smallegange
 et al., 2019), and it happens that authors interested in these taxa are
 also interested in bridging the gap in research between demographic modelling
 and agency with respect to development shaping demography (Smallegange
 ).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Developmental windows and sensitive periods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Demography, agency and life-history
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Hypothesis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Methods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
The Model Outline
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We model a large population of asexual individuals, distributed in a metapopulat
ion across habitat patches of equal size and quality, but each with a population
 density state with transition probabilities determined by the local population'
s growth rate.
 Each individual has three state dimensions—size (
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

), patch information (
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

), and reproductive quality (
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

)—upon which their developmental strategy is dependent, 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

, which determines the fraction of ingested energy allocated to developing
 greater reproductive quality over growth to increase their size state (and
 so ultimately the local population growth rate).
 Size determines whether or not an individual is mature given they can only
 reproduce when they reach their maximum size 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

.
 An individual's patch information state gives them the subjective probabilities
 regarding each possible population density state of their habitat patch.
 Finally, an individual's reproductive quality state 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

 determines their rate of reproduction at maturity.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We assume mutations are rare and of small effect such that effectively all
 individuals across the metapopulation have the same state-dependent development
al strategy (henceforth referred to as the resident strategy).
 Given the resident strategy we then do a forward projection using a Markov
 chain until demographic stability is reached at the metapopulation level
 (i.e.
 among patches).
 Given the probability distribution of population density states of patches,
 and assuming individuals have complete information as to this probability
 distribution and of the transition probabilities between patch pop.
 density states, we find the best response strategy for an invading individual
 (i.e.
 the developmental strategy that maximises their expected fitness).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
The Life Cycle and Demographic Feedback
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We model the life cycle of both individuals and of the patches they reside
 in (see figure 1).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Newborns disperse into random patches, with accurate prior information as
 to the probability distribution over patch population density states, and
 causing transitions in patch population density states.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individuals forage, with food availability being a random variable, normally
 distributed, with mean determined by the population density.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individuals update their patch information state given informativeness of
 food availability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Immature individuals allocate a proportion of their ingested energy towards
 increasing their future reproductive quality given their developmental
 strategy, the rest of the energy goes towards maturation (increasing their
 size).
 Mature individuals do not grow, inesting all ingested energy towards current
 reproduction.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individuals die given a baseline probability of death from exogenous factors
 and given perturbations in patch quality (e.g.
 disease outbreaks) influenced by the patch population density state, and
 in turn causing transitions of patch states to lower population densities.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Individuals that are of maximum size are mature and reproduce at rate determined
 by their reproductive quality state.
\end_layout

