\begin_layout Subsection
State dimensions
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Patch population size state, 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}\in\{q_{0},q_{1},...,q_{N}\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Size state, 
\begin_inset Formula $s\in(0,S)$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $S$
\end_inset

 is their size at maturity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Patch information state, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=\{P(q_{0}),P(q_{1}),...,P(q_{N})\}$
\end_inset

, where each position 
\begin_inset Formula $P(q_{i})\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{q_{i}}P(q_{i})=1$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Reproductive quality state, 
\begin_inset Formula $m\in(0,1).$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Demography
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Here we describe pseudocode for calculating the demographically stable probabili
ty distribution of population densities across patches and of the transition
 probabilities between patch density states.
 Patches have a population size state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}\in\{q_{0},q_{1},...,q_{N}\}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is the fineness of the grid of possible population sizes.
 The actual population size for any given state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{i}=c\cdot\frac{i}{N}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 is the maximum population size (or carrying capacity?) of any patch.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Reproduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Adults, individuals of size state 
\begin_inset Formula $s=S$
\end_inset

, reproduce asexually at a rate dependent on their reproductive quality
 state.
 We here define 
\begin_inset Formula $R(x(s,\pi,m))$
\end_inset

, the expected reproductive rate of an individual of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

 in a patch of density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

, as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
R(x(s,\pi,m))=\begin{cases}
\text{if \ensuremath{s\geq S}}: & r_{b}+m\cdot r_{\text{fighter}}\\
\text{else}: & 0
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

where S is their size at maturity, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{b}$
\end_inset

 is the baseline reproductive rate, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{fighter}$
\end_inset

 is the maximum benefit of reproductive quality boosting phenotypes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Dispersal
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
State distribution of newborns
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals are born with accurate prior information regarding the propbability
 distribution over the population density states of patches 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi=\{p(q_{0})=h(q_{0}),p(q_{1})=h(q_{1}),...,p(q_{N})=h(q_{N})\}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals then update their information state given cues for the population
 density of their patch which come from the availability of food, see section
 1.2.
 Individuals are born at size state 
\begin_inset Formula $0$
\end_inset

 and reproductive quality state 0.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Decision to disperse extension
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Re-enter dispersal pool given dispersal policy 
\begin_inset Formula $z(s,\pi,m)\in\{0,1\}$
\end_inset

, where they disperse if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $z(s,\pi,m)=1$
\end_inset

.
 If individuals choose to disperse they re-enter the dispersal pool with
 probability distribution over patch population density states being 
\begin_inset Formula $h(q_{i})$
\end_inset

, and so the number of individuals who either stay in or disperse into patches
 of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\delta(q_{i})=q_{i}\cdot h(q_{i})\cdot\sum_{s}\sum_{\pi}\sum_{m}[\rho_{i}(s,\pi,m)\cdot(1-z(s,\pi,m))]+\sum_{q_{j}}h(q_{j})\cdot q_{j}\cdot\sum_{s}\sum_{\pi}\sum_{m}z(s,\pi,m)\cdot\rho_{j}(s,\pi,m)
\]

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{i}(s,\pi,m)=\sum
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
as 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta(q_{i})$
\end_inset

 is a continuous variable it shall need to be interpolated back on to the
 grid to find the posterior patch probability distribution 
\begin_inset Formula $h(q_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Patch Projection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming a dispersal rate of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mu$
\end_inset

, the number of individuals arriving in each patch of population density
 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\alpha(q_{i})=(1-\mu)\cdot q_{i}\cdot r(q_{i})+\mu\cdot\sum_{j=0}^{N}h(q_{j})\cdot q_{j}\cdot r(q_{j})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $r(q)$
\end_inset

 is the expected reproductive rate of an individual in a patch of popuation
 density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 and where 
\begin_inset Formula $h(q_{i})$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of patches that are of density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 The expected reproductive rate of an individual in a patch of popuation
 density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is defined as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
r(q_{i})=\sum_{s}^{S}\sum_{\pi}^{\Pi}\sum_{m}^{M}[\rho_{i}(s,\pi,m)\cdot R(x(s,\pi,m),q_{i})]
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho_{i}(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

 is the probability that an individual will be of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

 given that they are in a patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $R(x(s,\pi,m))$
\end_inset

 is the reproductive rate of an individual of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

 in a patch of density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 (see section 1.4).
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Following arrivals of newborns and dispersal of juveniles and adults, the
 new population density of patches of prior density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 become 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{i}^{\prime}=\delta(q_{i})+\alpha(q_{i})
\]

\end_inset

We must then interpolate the posterior population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 back on to the grid of density states, with the nearest positions being
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d_{1}(i)=floor(\frac{q_{i}^{\prime}\cdot N}{c})
\]

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
d_{2}(i)=d_{1}(i)+1.
\]

\end_inset

Therefore patches of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 transition to patches of density state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{d_{2}(i)}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{2}(i)=\frac{q_{i}^{\prime}\cdot N}{c}-d_{1}(i),
\]

\end_inset

and transition to density state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{d_{1}(i)}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{1}(i)=1-p_{2}(i).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This then returns the transition probabilities between patch density states
 given dispersal 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{d_{1}(i)}|q_{i})$
\end_inset

, which at demographic stability will be used for finding the best response
 strategy.
 For all 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{d_{1}(i)}|q_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 and then
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Add 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}(i)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{d_{1}(i)}|q_{i})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Add 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}(i)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{births}(q_{d_{2}(i)}|q_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The posterior probability distribution over patch population density states
 is then defined as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h^{\prime}(q_{i})=\sum_{j}h(q_{j})\cdot p_{births}(q_{i}|q_{j})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Population Projection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So that we can track the growth rate of patches we must know the probability
 distribution of individual states within patches of each given size, so
 for patches of size 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 (to remind you) we define distribution over all possible state combinations
 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{i}(s,\pi,m)\text{: the proportion of individuals in patches of size \ensuremath{q_{i}} that are of state \ensuremath{\{s,\pi,m\}}}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals in any patch state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 will, given arrivals from reproduction, transition to patch state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{d_{1}(i)}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{1}(i)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{d_{2}(i)}$
\end_inset

 with probability 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{2}(i)$
\end_inset

.
 Arrivals will also be added, with the posterior distribution as weighted
 by the change in population size thusly
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{d_{1}(i)}^{\prime}(s,\pi,m)=p(remainers|i)\cdot\rho_{i}(s,\pi,m)+\frac{d_{1}(i)-i}{d_{1}(i)}\cdot(\frac{\alpha}{\alpha+\delta}\cdot\tilde{\rho}(s,\pi,m)+\frac{\delta}{\alpha+\delta})
\]

\end_inset

and 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{d_{2}(i)}^{\prime}(s,\pi,m)=\frac{i}{d_{2}(i)}\cdot\rho_{i}(s,\pi,m)+\frac{d_{2}(i)-i}{d_{2}(i)}\cdot\tilde{\rho}(s,\pi,m)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{\rho}(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

for a newborn (as all arrivals are).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Foraging and Nutrition cues
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Individuals observe cues for population density in the form of food availability.
 Food availability is a random variable influenced by the population density,
 all patches produce the same amount of food which must be shared, so for
 any given patch of population density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 individuals receive a food mass of 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f\sim\text{Normal}(\frac{i}{N},\sigma_{food})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{food}$
\end_inset

 is the food mass standard deviation.
 However we must approximate this normal distribution using a pseudo-normal
 probability distribution over a grid possible food mass values 
\begin_inset Formula $F\in\{f_{0},f_{1},...,f_{G}\}$
\end_inset

, with probabilies for each realised food mass being 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
p(f_{g}|p(q_{i}))=\frac{1}{K}e^{-\frac{(f_{g}/N)^{2}}{2\sigma_{food}^{2}}} & \text{for} & f_{g}\in F,\end{array}\label{eq:-2}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is a suitable integer that determines the fineness of the grid of values,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
K=\underset{-G}{\overset{G}{\boldsymbol{\sum}}}e^{-\frac{(f_{g}/N)^{2}}{2\sigma_{food}^{2}}}.
\]

\end_inset

Given the food mass 
\begin_inset Formula $f_{g}$
\end_inset

, individuals observe the resulting nutrient data and update their population
 density information states.
 Here we find the transition probability of individuals given these observations
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(p^{\prime}(q_{j})|p(q_{i}),f_{g})=\frac{p(q_{i})\cdot p(f_{g}|p(q_{i}))}{\sum_{k}p(q_{k})\cdot p(f_{g}|p(q_{k}))}
\]

\end_inset

 and so posterior distribution of states amongst individuals in each patch
 is 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall q_{i}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{q_{i}}^{\prime\prime}(s,\pi^{\prime},m)=\sum_{s}\sum_{m}\sum_{\pi}\sum_{g}p(\pi^{\prime}|\pi,f_{g})\cdot\rho_{q_{i}}^{\prime}(s,\pi,m)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Development
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Each individual, choose a developmental action given their state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

, given the resident developmental strategy for that state 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,\pi,m)\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Their developmental action determines the proportion of energy foraged
 that goes to the development of phenotypes of greater reproductive quality
 (e.g.
 fighter morphology), generating a transition in their size state 
\begin_inset Formula $s$
\end_inset

 and their reproductive quality state 
\begin_inset Formula $m$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\rho_{i}^{\prime\prime}(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime})=\sum_{s}\sum_{\pi}\sum_{m}p(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi,m)\cdot\rho_{i}^{\prime}(s,\pi,m)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

 is the transition probability between state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime}\}$
\end_inset

 and state 
\begin_inset Formula $\{s,\pi,m\}$
\end_inset

 given the resident developmental strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $x(s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

, which is found be doing the following, for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 and all 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, add 
\begin_inset Formula $p(f_{g}(i))$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s^{\prime},\pi^{\prime},m^{\prime}|s,\pi,m)$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s^{\prime}=s+max(0,(1-x(s,\pi,m))\cdot g)-y(s)),
\]

\end_inset

is the posterior size state of individuals after ingesting a food mass of
 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

, given that all energy allocated to growth below a certain threshold 
\begin_inset Formula $y(s)$
\end_inset

, always goes to maintenance and 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
m^{\prime}=m+x(s,\pi,m)\cdot g
\]

\end_inset

is the posterior quality state of individuals after ingesting a food mass
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Maintenance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Of the portion of energy allocated to growth, all energy below a size-dependent
 threshold goes to maintenance costs.
 Therefore, if allocation to growth falls below this threshold there is
 a cost to survival 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(survival|s,\pi,m,g)=\frac{min(y(s),(1-x(s,\pi,m))\cdot g))}{y(s)},
\]

\end_inset

such that if the individual has no energy to allocate to maintenance they
 die.
 The posterior distribution of patch states is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h^{\hat{\prime}}(q_{i})=\sum_{q_{j}}h(q_{j})\cdot p(q_{i}|q_{j})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(q_{i}=s_{1}(j)|q_{j})=p_{1}(j)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(q_{i}=s_{2}(j)|q_{j})=p_{2}(j)
\]

\end_inset

 and where 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{1}(j)=floor(\frac{N\cdot q_{j}\cdot\sum_{s}\sum_{g}(P(survival|s,\pi,m,g)\cdot p(g|q_{j})\cdot p(s|q_{i}))}{c})
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
s_{2}(j)=s_{1}(j)+1
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{2}(j)=\frac{N\cdot q_{j}\cdot\sum_{s}\sum_{\pi}\sum_{m}\sum_{g}(P(survival|s,\pi,m,g)\cdot p(g|q_{j})\cdot p(s|q_{i}))}{c}-s_{1}
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{1}(j)=1-p_{2}(j)
\]

\end_inset

and so run the following algoritm for each 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{j}$
\end_inset

:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
For all 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $p(q_{i}|q_{j})=0$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Add 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{\prime}(q_{j})\cdot p_{1}(j)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s_{1}(q_{j})|q_{j})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Add 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{\prime}(q_{j})\cdot p_{2}(j)$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $p(s_{2}(q_{j})|q_{j})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The posterior distribution of individual states for each given patch population
 density state is therefore 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\rho_{q_{i}}^{\prime}}(s,\pi,m)=\sum_{q_{j}}h^{\prime}(q_{j})\cdot p(q_{i}|q_{j})\cdot\frac{\rho_{q_{j}}^{\prime}(s,\pi,m)\cdot\sum_{g}(P(survival|s,\pi,m,g)\cdot p(g|q_{j})}{\sum_{s}\sum_{\pi}\sum_{m}[\rho_{q_{j}}^{\prime}(s,\pi,m)\cdot\sum_{g}(P(survival|s,\pi,m,g)\cdot p(g|q_{j})]}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
probability distribution over posterior population sizes is given by Bernoulli
 trial for each individual and so binomial for the population size of individual
s of each state
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
Binomial(n=q_{i}\cdot\rho_{i}^{\prime}(s,\pi,m),p=\sum_{g}p(g|q_{i})\cdot P(survival|s,\pi,m,g).
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Death
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Every patch has some probability of 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

depletion
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 where perturbations in patch quality, influenced by patch population density
 (e.g.
 the local extinction of a prey species), and demographic stochasticity
 result in enough deaths such that patches can drop probabilisitically in
 their population density state.
 We assume patches rapidly return to normal quality after depletion and
 we assume that the probability of death is equal for all individuals so
 there is no change in the distribution of individual states within patches.
 We assume these perturbations have a normal distribution, although once
 again we must approximate this normal distribution to interpolate transition
 probabilities on to the grid of possible density states, where corresponding
 to the range of possible population density transitions we define the set
 
\begin_inset Formula $T=\{-N,-N+1,...,N-1,N\}$
\end_inset

, with a pseudo-normal distribution over this set defining the probability
 of any given possible change in density 
\begin_inset Formula $\tau\in T$
\end_inset

, defined as
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{ccc}
p(\tau)=\frac{1}{K_{\gamma}}e^{-\frac{(\tau/N)^{2}}{2\sigma_{\gamma}^{2}}} & \text{for} & \tau\in T,\end{array}\label{eq:-2-1}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{\gamma}^{2}$
\end_inset

 is the standard deviation parameter for perturbations and 
\begin_inset Formula $N$
\end_inset

 is a suitable integer that determines the fineness of the grid of values,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $K_{\gamma}$
\end_inset

 is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
K_{\gamma}=\underset{-N}{\overset{N}{\boldsymbol{\sum}}}e^{-\frac{(\tau/N)^{2}}{2\sigma_{\gamma}^{2}}}.
\]

\end_inset

 The shift in probability generated by demographic feedback is defined as
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c=round(\frac{q_{i}}{q_{N}}\cdot\gamma)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 is the maximum risk of mass mortality events associated with population
 density.
 we can then define 
\begin_inset Formula $p_{deaths}(q_{i}|q_{j})$
\end_inset

, the transition probability of a patch of density j to a density of i as
 a result of depletion
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p_{deaths}(q_{i}|q_{j})=\begin{cases}
\text{if }i=0 & \sum_{\tau=min(N,i+c-j)}^{\tau=-N}p(\tau)\\
\text{if }0<i<N & p(\tau=min(N,i+c-j)\\
\text{if }i=N & \sum_{\tau=min(N,i+c-j)}^{\tau=N}p(\tau)
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Given these perturbations, the proportion of patches that are of population
 density 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 for all 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 in the next time step is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h^{\prime\prime}(q_{i})=\sum_{j}^{N}p_{deaths}(q_{i}|q_{j})\cdot h^{\prime}(q_{j})
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Demographic Stability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assuming that with successive generations 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 that the distribution of patches over states converges on stable values,
 i.e.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall q_{i}$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\text{\ensuremath{h^{*}(q_{i})}=lim}_{\rightarrow\infty}[h(q_{i})]
\]

\end_inset

further, we can approximate 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{*}(q_{i})$
\end_inset

 by iterating over generations until 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
tol>\sum_{i}|h(q_{i})-h^{\prime\prime}(q_{i})|
\]

\end_inset

where the tolerance threshold, 
\begin_inset Formula $tol$
\end_inset

, is very small, and 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{\prime\prime}(q_{i})$
\end_inset

 is the proportion of patches that are of population density state 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 after dispersal and deaths.
 Once demographic stability is reached we assume 
\begin_inset Formula $h^{*}(q_{i})=h(q_{i})$
\end_inset

 and proceed with invasion analysis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Invasion Analysis
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Having found the demographically stable state of the metapopulation, given
 the resident strategy, we can then use the Bellman equation to find the
 best response strategy 
\begin_inset Formula $b(x(s,\pi,m))$
\end_inset

 for an invading individual.
 We then assume the best response strategy becomes the new resident strategy,
 and recalculate the demographically stable state of the metapopulation.
 The evolutionarily stable strategy (ESS) is a resident strategy for which
 invading individuals can do no better than to follow the resident strategy
 and so the ESS is defined as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\bar{x}(s,\pi,m)=b(x(s,\pi,m))=x(s,\pi,m).
\]

\end_inset

Assuming there is a singular ESSs, we can iterating over invasions till
 the best response strategy converges on the ESS, and the starting point
 of the population will not matter.
 By sampling from multiple starting points we can test this assumption,
 and if necessary plot out the adaptive dynamics with phase portraits where
 there are multiple ESSs or oscillatory dynamics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
To find the best response we define a Bellman fitness equation for individuals
 in the model
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
V_{n+1}(s,\pi,m)=\text{max}_{\kappa}[R(x(s,\pi,m)=\kappa)+\lambda\sum_{s_{n}}\sum_{\pi_{n}}\sum_{m_{n}}p(s_{n},\pi_{n},m_{n}|s,\pi,m,\kappa)\cdot V_{n}(s_{n},\pi_{n},m_{n})],
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 is the baseline probability of death between timesteps, and where 
\begin_inset Formula $V_{n=0}(s,\pi,m)=0$
\end_inset

 for all state combinations and where the transition probabilites are defined
 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(s_{n},\pi_{n},m_{n}|s,\pi,m,\kappa)=P(s_{n},m_{n}|s,\pi_{n},m,\kappa)\cdot P(\pi_{n}|\pi)
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $P(s_{n},m_{n}|s,\pi_{n},m,\kappa)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of developing into states 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{n}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $m_{n}$
\end_inset

 given their current somatic state and given the expected food availability
 given their updated information state, and where 
\begin_inset Formula $P(\pi_{n}|\pi)$
\end_inset

 is the probability of a posterior patch information state given the transition
 probabilities in population densities for the resident population strategy
 at demographic stability and given cues from observations of food availability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This then returns the final transition probabilities between patch density
 states given births and deaths 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
p(q_{j}|q_{i})=\sum_{k}p_{deaths}(q_{j}|q_{k})\cdot p_{births}(q_{k}|q_{i}).
\]

\end_inset

